-> outside_the_walls

=== outside_the_walls ===
Jonah leaves the city and sits east of it, waiting to see what will become of Nineveh — as if forty days might still come due.
"I pray thee, O Lord, was not this my saying, when I was yet in my country? I knew that thou art a gracious God, and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness."
"Therefore now, O Lord, take, I beseech thee, my life from me; for it is better for me to die than to live."
-> the_plant

=== the_plant ===
The Lord God prepares a plant, and makes it come up over Jonah, to be a shadow over his head, to deliver him from his grief. Jonah is exceeding glad of the plant.
-> the_worm

=== the_worm ===
But when the morning rises the next day, God prepares a worm, and it smites the plant, that it withers.
A vehement east wind rises. The sun beats on Jonah's head until he faints, and wishes in himself to die.
"It is better for me to die than to live."
-> gods_question

=== gods_question ===
"Doest thou well to be angry for the plant?"
"I do well to be angry, even unto death."
"Thou hast had pity on the plant, for the which thou hast not laboured, neither madest it grow; which came up in a night, and perished in a night."
"And should not I spare Nineveh, that great city, wherein are more than sixscore thousand persons that cannot discern between their right hand and their left hand; and also much cattle?"
* [Silence]
    -> silence_ending
* [Anger]
    -> anger_ending
* [A real question]
    -> question_ending

=== silence_ending ===
Jonah says nothing more. The text ends here — no rebuttal, no resolution. Just the question, left standing.
-> END

=== anger_ending ===
"It is not right. I did not ask for mercy on their behalf. I asked for justice."
The question remains unanswered by an answer that satisfies him.
-> END

=== question_ending ===
"...Why does it cost you nothing to spare them, and everything to spare me?"
No answer comes — only the wind, and the shadow of a plant that is no longer there.
-> END
